          %h2#idea Idea
          %p
            The concept of
            %em crossed modules
            of groups (
            %a{:href => "#Whitehead41"}> Whitehead 41
            ,
            = succeed ")" do
              %a{:href => "#Whitehead49"} Whitehead 49
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopical+algebra"} homotopical algebra
            and
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homological+algebra"} homological algebra
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/nPOV"} nPOV
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/strict+2-group"} strict 2-group
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi G
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
            in terms of a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homomorphism"} homomorphism
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo ∂
                  %mo :
                  %msub
                    %mi G
                    %mn 2
                  %mo →
                  %msub
                    %mi G
                    %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \partial : G_2 \to G_1
            of two ordinary
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/groups"} groups
          %p From other points of view it is:
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                like the inclusion of a
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/normal+subgroup"} normal subgroup
            %li
              %p
                like a
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/module"} module
                with a twisted ‘multiplication’;
            %li
              %p
                like an
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/action"} action
                by
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/automorphisms"} automorphisms
                on a group;
            %li
              %p
                a
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/crossed+complex"} crossed complex
                concentrated in degrees
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mn 1
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 1
                and
                = succeed ";" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mn 2
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 2
            %li
              %p
                a nonabelian
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/chain+complex"} chain-complex
                of length 2;
            %li
              %p
                a
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Moore+complex"} Moore complex
                of certain
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/simplicial+groups"} simplicial groups
          %p
            Historically, crossed modules were among the first examples of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/higher+dimensional+algebra"} higher dimensional algebra
            to be studied.
          %h2#definition Definition
          %h3#diagrammatic_definition Diagrammatic definition
          %p
          #DiagrammaticDefinition.num_defn
            %h6 Definition
            %p
              A
              %strong crossed module of groups
              is
            %ul
              %li
                %p
                  a
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/pair"} pair
                  of
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/groups"} groups
                  = succeed "," do
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %msub
                            %mi G
                            %mn 2
                          %mo ,
                          %msub
                            %mi G
                            %mn 1
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G_2, G_1
              %li
                %p
                  a
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/group+homomorphism"} group homomorphism
                .maruku-equation
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi G
                          %mn 2
                        %mover
                          %mo ⟶
                          %mi δ
                        %msub
                          %mi G
                          %mn 1
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                        G_2
                        \\overset{
                        \\delta
                        }{
                        \\longrightarrow
                        }
                        G_1
              %li
                %p
                  a
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/group+homomorphism"} group homomorphism
                  from
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi G
                          %mn 1
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G_1
                  to the
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/automorphism+group"} automorphism group
                  of
                  = succeed ":" do
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %msub
                            %mi G
                            %mn 2
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G_2
                .maruku-equation
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi G
                          %mn 1
                        %mover
                          %mo ⟶
                          %mi α
                        %mi Aut
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %msub
                          %mi G
                          %mn 2
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                        %mo ,
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                        G_1
                        \\overset{
                        \\alpha
                        }{
                        \\longrightarrow
                        }
                        Aut(G_2)
                        \\,,
                %p
                  which we may equivalently regard as a
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/function"} function
                .maruku-equation
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi α
                        %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                        %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                        %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                        %msub
                          %mi G
                          %mn 1
                        %mo ×
                        %msub
                          %mi G
                          %mn 2
                        %mo ⟶
                        %msub
                          %mi G
                          %mn 2
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                        \\alpha
                        \\;\colon\;
                        G_1 \times G_2
                        \\longrightarrow
                        G_2
                %p
                  (out of the
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Cartesian+product"} Cartesian product
                  of
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/underlying"} underlying
                  = succeed "/" do
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sets"} sets
                  = succeed ")" do
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/objects"} objects
                %p
                  that satisfies the
                  = succeed "-" do
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %msub
                            %mi G
                            %mn 1
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G_1
                  %a{:href => "action#eq:ActionProperty"} action property
                  and is such that for every
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi g
                          %mn 1
                        %mo ∈
                        %msub
                          %mi G
                          %mn 1
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} g_1 \in G_1
                  it is a group
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/automorphism"} automorphism
                  of
                  = succeed ";" do
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %msub
                            %mi G
                            %mn 2
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G_2
            %p
              such that the following
              = succeed ":" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/commuting+diagram"} diagrams commute
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mrow
                      %mtable
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                            %msub
                              %mi G
                              %mn 2
                            %mo ×
                            %msub
                              %mi G
                              %mn 2
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                            %mover
                              %mo ⟶
                              %mrow
                                %mi δ
                                %mo ×
                                %mi Id
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                            %msub
                              %mi G
                              %mn 1
                            %mo ×
                            %msub
                              %mi G
                              %mn 2
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                            %msub
                              %mrow
                              %mpadded{:lspace => "-100%width", :width => "0"}
                                %mi Ad
                            %mo ↘
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                            %msub
                              %mo ↙
                              %mpadded{:width => "0"}
                                %mi α
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                            %msub
                              %mi G
                              %mn 2
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    \\array{
                    G_2 \times G_2
                    \&amp;&amp;
                    \\overset{
                    \\delta \times Id
                    }{
                    \\longrightarrow
                    }
                    \&amp;&amp;
                    G_1 \times G_2
                    \\\
                    \&amp;
                    {}_{\mathllap{Ad}}
                    \\searrow
                    \&amp;&amp;
                    \\swarrow_{\mathrlap{\alpha}}
                    \\\
                    \&amp;&amp;
                    G_2
                    }
            %p and
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mrow
                      %mtable
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                            %msub
                              %mi G
                              %mn 1
                            %mo ×
                            %msub
                              %mi G
                              %mn 2
                          %mtd
                            %mover
                              %mo ⟶
                              %mi α
                          %mtd
                            %msub
                              %mi G
                              %mn 2
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                            %mo{:maxsize => "1.2em", :minsize => "1.2em"} ↓
                            %mpadded{:width => "0"}
                              %mrow
                                %msup
                                  %mrow
                                  %mrow
                                    %mi Id
                                    %mo ×
                                    %mi δ
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                            %mo{:maxsize => "1.2em", :minsize => "1.2em"} ↓
                            %mpadded{:width => "0"}
                              %mrow
                                %msup
                                  %mrow
                                  %mi δ
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                            %msub
                              %mi G
                              %mn 1
                            %mo ×
                            %msub
                              %mi G
                              %mn 1
                          %mtd
                            %mover
                              %mo ⟶
                              %mi Ad
                          %mtd
                            %msub
                              %mi G
                              %mn 1
                            %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                            %mo ,
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    \\array{
                    G_1 \times G_2
                    \&amp;
                    \\stackrel{
                    \\alpha
                    }{
                    \\longrightarrow
                    }
                    \&amp;
                    G_2
                    \\\
                    \\big\downarrow\mathrlap{{}^{
                    {Id \times \delta}
                    }}
                    \&amp;&amp;
                    \\big\downarrow\mathrlap{{}^{
                    {\delta}
                    }}
                    \\\
                    G_1 \times G_1
                    \&amp;
                    \\stackrel{
                    Ad
                    }{
                    \\longrightarrow
                    }
                    \&amp;
                    G_1
                    \\,,
                    }
            %p
              where
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Ad
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Ad
              denotes the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/adjoint+action"} adjoint action
              of
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi G
                      %mn 2
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G_2
              on itself.
          %p
          .num_remark
            %h6 Remark
            %p
              The diagrammatic Def.
              %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#DiagrammaticDefinition"}
              makes sense
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/internalization"} internal
              to any
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cartesian+monoidal+category"} cartesian monoidal category
              = succeed ":" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi 𝒞
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{C}
            %ul
              %li
                %p
                  for
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi 𝒞
                        %mo =
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{C}=
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Sets"} Sets
                  we get bare crossed modules of
                  = succeed ";" do
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/discrete+groups"} discrete groups
              %li
                %p
                  for
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi 𝒞
                        %mo =
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{C}=
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/SmoothManifolds"} SmoothManifolds
                  we get crossed modules of
                  = succeed ";" do
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lie+groups"} Lie groups
                %p
                  if one allows
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/infinite-dimensional+manifolds"} infinite-dimensional manifolds
                  here then this subsumes crossed modules of
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Kac-Moody+groups"} Kac-Moody groups
                  such as appear in models for the
                  = succeed "." do
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/String+2-group"} String 2-group
            %p
          %p
            Alternatively, one can take another tack, and define crossed module objects in categories that support enough structure without using internal groups, the most general case of which, in practice, are
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/semiabelian+categories"} semiabelian categories
            = succeed ")" do
              %a{:href => "#Janelidze_03"} Janelidze 2003
          %p
            A key result, also due to (
            %a{:href => "#Janelidze_03"}> Janelidze 2003
            ) and generalising the Brown-Spencer theorem from the case of ordinary crossed modules, is the following:
          .num_theorem
            %h6#theorem Theorem
            %p
              %strong (Janelidze’s Brown-Spencer theorem).
              Let
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi C
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
              be a
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/semiabelian+category"} semiabelian category
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi XMod
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi C
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} XMod(C)
              of crossed modules in
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi C
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
              is equivalent to the category
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Gpd
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi C
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Gpd(C)
              of internal groupoids in
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi C
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
          %p
            Here the notion of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/internal+groupoid"} internal groupoid
            is the usual diagrammatic notion.
          %h3#definition_in_terms_of_equations Definition in terms of equations
          %p
            The two
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/diagrams"} diagrams
            can be translated into
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equations"} equations
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                If we write the effect of acting with
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi g
                        %mn 1
                      %mo ∈
                      %msub
                        %mi G
                        %mn 1
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} g_1\in G_1
                on
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi g
                        %mn 2
                      %mo ∈
                      %msub
                        %mi G
                        %mn 2
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} g_2\in G_2
                as
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %msup
                          %mrow
                          %mrow
                            %msub
                              %mi g
                              %mn 1
                        %msub
                          %mi g
                          %mn 2
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} {}^{g_1}g_2
              .maruku-equation
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi δ
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %msup
                        %mrow
                        %mrow
                          %msub
                            %mi g
                            %mn 1
                      %msub
                        %mi g
                        %mn 2
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo =
                      %msub
                        %mi g
                        %mn 1
                      %mi δ
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %msub
                        %mi g
                        %mn 2
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %msubsup
                        %mi g
                        %mn 1
                        %mrow
                          %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                          %mn 1
                      %mo .
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \delta({}^{g_1}g_2) = g_1\delta(g_2)g_1^{-1}.
              %p
                In other words,
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi δ
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \delta
                is equivariant for the action of
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi G
                          %mn 1
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G_1
            %li
              %p
                The first diagram is slightly more subtle. The group
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi G
                        %mn 2
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G_2
                can act on itself in two different ways, (i) by the usual conjugation action,
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msup
                        %mrow
                        %mrow
                          %msub
                            %mi g
                            %mn 2
                      %msubsup
                        %mi g
                        %mn 2
                        %mstyle{:scriptlevel => "0"}
                          %mo ′
                      %mo =
                      %msub
                        %mi g
                        %mn 2
                      %msubsup
                        %mi g
                        %mn 2
                        %mstyle{:scriptlevel => "0"}
                          %mo ′
                      %msubsup
                        %mi g
                        %mn 2
                        %mrow
                          %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                          %mn 1
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} {}^{g_2}g^\prime_2=g_2g^\prime_2g_2^{-1}
                and (ii) by first mapping
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi g
                        %mn 2
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} g_2
                down to
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi G
                        %mn 1
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G_1
                and then using the action of that group back on
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi G
                          %mn 2
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G_2
          #eq:PeifferRule.maruku-equation
            %span.maruku-eq-number (1)
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mrow
                    %mrow
                      %mi δ
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %msub
                        %mi g
                        %mn 2
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %msubsup
                    %mi g
                    %mn 2
                    %mstyle{:scriptlevel => "0"}
                      %mo ′
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mo =
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %msub
                    %mi g
                    %mn 2
                  %msubsup
                    %mi g
                    %mn 2
                    %mstyle{:scriptlevel => "0"}
                      %mo ′
                  %msubsup
                    %mi g
                    %mn 2
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                      %mn 1
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  {}^{\delta(g_2)}g^\prime_2
                  \\;=\;
                  g_2g^\prime_2g_2^{-1}
                  \\,.
          %p
            This equation is known as the
            %strong Peiffer rule
            in the literature.
          %p Another way to interpret it is to rewrite it slightly:
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mrow
                    %mrow
                      %mi δ
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %msub
                        %mi g
                        %mn 2
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %msubsup
                    %mi g
                    %mn 2
                    %mstyle{:scriptlevel => "0"}
                      %mo ′
                  %msub
                    %mi g
                    %mn 2
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mo =
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %msub
                    %mi g
                    %mn 2
                  %msubsup
                    %mi g
                    %mn 2
                    %mstyle{:scriptlevel => "0"}
                      %mo ′
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  {}^{\delta(g_2)}g^\prime_2 g_2
                  \\;=\;
                  g_2g^\prime_2
                  \\,.
          %p
            The Peiffer rule can thus be seen as a “twisted commutativity law” for
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi G
                      %mn 2
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G_2
          %h3#Morphisms Morphisms
          %p
            For
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi G
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi H
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} H
            two
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/strict+2-group"} strict 2-groups
            coming from crossed modules
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mi G
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [G]
            and
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %mi H
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [H]
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi f
                    %mo :
                    %mi G
                    %mo →
                    %mi H
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f : G \to H
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mi f
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %mo :
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mi G
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %mo →
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mi H
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [f] : [G] \to [H]
            is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/2-functor"} 2-functor
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                    %mi B
                  %mi f
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                    %mi B
                  %mi G
                  %mo →
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                    %mi B
                  %mi H
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\mathbf{B}f \colon \mathbf{B}G \to \mathbf{B}H
          %p
            between the corresponding
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/delooping"} delooped
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/2-groupoid"} 2-groupoids
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mi G
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [G]
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mi H
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [H]
            yields a diagram called a
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/butterfly"} butterfly
          %h2#properties Properties
          %p
          #KernelOfDeltaIsCentral.num_lemma
            %h6 Lemma
            %p
              Given a crossed module
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi H
                    %mover
                      %mo →
                      %mi δ
                    %mi G
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} H \xrightarrow{\delta} G
              Def.
              = succeed "," do
                %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#DiagrammaticDefinition"}
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/kernel"} kernel
              of
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi δ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \delta
              is inside the
              %a{:href => "center#OfGroupsAndMonoids"} center
              of
              = succeed ":" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi H
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} H
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ker
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi δ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ⊂
                    %mi Z
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi H
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mo .
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    ker(\delta) \subset Z(H)
                    \\,.
            %p
          .proof
            %h6 Proof
            %p
              For
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi h
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi ker
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi δ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ⊂
                    %mi H
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} h \in ker(\delta) \subset H
              and
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi h
                    %mo ′
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi H
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} h' \in H
              we have
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mtable{:columnalign => "right left left", :displaystyle => "false", :rowspacing => "0.5ex"}
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mi h
                          %mi h
                          %mo ′
                          %msup
                            %mi h
                            %mrow
                              %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                              %mn 1
                        %mtd
                          %mo ≡
                          %msub
                            %mi Ad
                            %mi h
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mi h
                          %mo ′
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %mo =
                          %mi α
                          %mo{:maxsize => "1.2em", :minsize => "1.2em"} (
                          %mi δ
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mi h
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                          %mo{:maxsize => "1.2em", :minsize => "1.2em"} )
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mi h
                          %mo ′
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mtd
                          %mtext by first axiom
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %mo =
                          %mi α
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} e
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mi h
                          %mo ′
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mtd
                          %mtext by assumption
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %mo =
                          %mi h
                          %mo ′
                        %mtd
                          %mtext by action property
                          %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                          %mo .
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    \\begin{array}{rll}
                    h h' h^{-1}
                    \&amp; \equiv Ad_h(h')
                    \\\
                    \&amp; = \alpha\big( \delta(h) \big)(h') &amp; \text{by first axiom}
                    \\\
                    \&amp; = \alpha( \mathrm{e} )(h') &amp; \text{by assumption}
                    \\\
                    \&amp; = h' &amp; \text{by action property}
                    \\,.
                    \\end{array}
            %p But this proves the claim, since:
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi h
                    %mi h
                    %mo ′
                    %msup
                      %mi h
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                        %mn 1
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mo =
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mi h
                    %mo ′
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mo ⇔
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mi h
                    %mi h
                    %mo ′
                    %mo =
                    %mi h
                    %mo ′
                    %mi h
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mo .
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    h h' h^{-1}
                    \\,=\,
                    h'
                    \\;\;\;\;\;\;
                    \\Leftrightarrow
                    \\;\;\;\;\;\;
                    h h' = h' h
                    \\,.
            %p
          %h2#examples Examples
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                For
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi H
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} H
                any group, its
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/automorphism"} automorphism
                crossed module is
              .maruku-equation
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi AUT
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi H
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo ≔
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %msub
                        %mi G
                        %mn 2
                      %mo =
                      %mi H
                      %mo ,
                      %msub
                        %mi G
                        %mn 1
                      %mo =
                      %mi Aut
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi H
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo ,
                      %mi δ
                      %mo =
                      %mi Ad
                      %mo ,
                      %mi α
                      %mo =
                      %mi Id
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                      %mo .
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                      AUT(H)
                      \\coloneqq
                      (G_2 = H, G_1 = Aut(H), \delta = Ad, \alpha = Id)
                      \\,.
              %p
                Under the equivalence of crossed modules with
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/strict+2-group"} strict 2-groups
                this corresponds to the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/automorphism+2-group"} automorphism 2-group
              .maruku-equation
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi Aut
                        %mi Grpd
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                        %mi B
                      %mi H
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                      Aut_{Grpd}(\mathbf{B}H)
              %p
                of
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/automorphism"} automorphisms
                in the category
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Grpd"} Grpd
                of
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/groupoid"} groupoids
                on the one-object
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/delooping"} delooping
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/groupoid"} groupoid
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                        %mi B
                      %mi H
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{B}H
                of
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi H
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} H
            %li
              %p
                Almost the canonical example of a crossed module is given by a group
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi G
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
                and a
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/normal+subgroup"} normal subgroup
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi N
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} N
                of
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi G
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi G
                          %mn 2
                        %mo =
                        %mi N
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G_2 = N
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi G
                        %mn 1
                      %mo =
                      %mi G
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G_1 = G
                with the action
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi α
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \alpha
                being the
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/conjugation+action"} conjugation action
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi δ
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \delta
                is the given inclusion,
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi N
                        %mo ↪
                        %mi G
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} N \hookrightarrow G
          %p
            This is ‘almost canonical’, since if we replace the groups by simplicial groups
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi G
                    %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G_.
            and
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi N
                      %mo .
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} N_.
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mi π
                    %mn 0
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mi G
                    %mo .
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ,
                  %msub
                    %mi π
                    %mn 0
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mi N
                    %mo .
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ,
                  %msub
                    %mi π
                    %mn 0
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi inc
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (\pi_0(G_.),\pi_0(N_.),\pi_0(inc))
            is a crossed module, and given any crossed module,
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi C
                    %mo ,
                    %mi P
                    %mo ,
                    %mi δ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (C,P,\delta)
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi G
                    %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G_.
            and a normal subgroup
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi N
                      %mo .
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} N_.
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                Another standard example of a crossed module is
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi M
                      %msup
                        %mo →
                        %mn 0
                      %mi P
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} M \to ^0 P
                where
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi P
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P
                is a group and
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi M
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} M
                is a
                = succeed "-module." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi P
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P
            %li
              %p
                If
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi μ
                      %mo :
                      %mi M
                      %mo →
                      %mi P
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mu: M \to P
                is a crossed module with cokernel
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi G
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi M
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} M
                is abelian, then the operation of
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi P
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P
                on
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi M
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} M
                factors through
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi G
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi M
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} M
                and
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi P
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P
                are abelian should not be sneezed at! A good example is
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi μ
                      %mo :
                      %msub
                        %mi C
                        %mn 2
                      %mo ×
                      %msub
                        %mi C
                        %mn 2
                      %mo →
                      %msub
                        %mi C
                        %mn 4
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mu: C_2 \times C_2 \to C_4
                where
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi C
                        %mi n
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C_n
                denotes the cyclic group of order
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi n
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi μ
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mu
                is injective on each factor, and
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi C
                        %mn 4
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C_4
                acts on the product by the twist. This crossed module has a
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/classifying+space"} classifying space
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi X
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
                with fundamental and second homotopy groups
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi C
                        %mn 2
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C_2
                and non trivial
                = succeed "-invariant" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi k
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} k
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %msup
                          %mi H
                          %mn 3
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %msub
                          %mi C
                          %mn 2
                        %mo ,
                        %msub
                          %mi C
                          %mn 2
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} H^3(C_2, C_2)
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi X
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
                is not a product of
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Eilenberg-MacLane+space"} Eilenberg-MacLane spaces
                = succeed "-invariant." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi k
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} k
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/higher+homotopy+van+Kampen+theorem"} higher homotopy van Kampen theorem
                implies that the above
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi X
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
                gives the 2-type of the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/mapping+cone"} mapping cone
                of the map of
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/classifying+space"} classifying spaces
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi BC
                          %mn 2
                        %mo →
                        %msub
                          %mi BC
                          %mn 4
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} BC_2 \to BC_4
            %li
              %p
                Suppose
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi F
                      %mover
                        %mo →
                        %mi i
                      %mi E
                      %mover
                        %mo →
                        %mi p
                      %mi B
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F\stackrel{i}{\to}E\stackrel{p}{\to}B
                is a
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fibration+sequence"} fibration sequence
              %p
                of
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/pointed+object"} pointed spaces
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi p
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p
                is a
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fibration"} fibration
                in the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/model+structure+on+topological+spaces"} topological sense
                (lifting of paths and homotopies of paths will suffice),
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi F
                        %mo =
                        %msup
                          %mi p
                          %mrow
                            %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                            %mn 1
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %msub
                          %mi b
                          %mn 0
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F = p^{-1}(b_0)
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi b
                        %mn 0
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} b_0
                is the basepoint of
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi B
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi F
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F
                is pointed at
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi f
                          %mn 0
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f_0
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi f
                        %mn 0
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f_0
                is taken as the basepoint of
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi E
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} E
                as well.
              %p
                There is an induced map on
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+group"} homotopy groups
              .maruku-equation
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi π
                        %mn 1
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi F
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mover
                        %mo ⟶
                        %mrow
                          %msub
                            %mi π
                            %mn 1
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mi i
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %msub
                        %mi π
                        %mn 1
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi E
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \pi_1(F) \stackrel{\pi_1(i)}{\longrightarrow} \pi_1(E)
              %p
                and if
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi a
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a
                is a loop in
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi E
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} E
                based at
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi f
                          %mn 0
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f_0
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi b
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} b
                a loop in
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi F
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F
                based at
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi f
                          %mn 0
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f_0
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi a
                      %mi b
                      %msup
                        %mi a
                        %mrow
                          %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                          %mn 1
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a b a^{-1}
                is
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy"} homotopic
                to one wholly within
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi F
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi p
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi a
                      %mi b
                      %msup
                        %mi a
                        %mrow
                          %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                          %mn 1
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p(a b a^{-1})
                is
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/null+homotopy"} null homotopic
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy"} homotopy
                in
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi B
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B
                between it and the constant map, then lift that homotopy back up to
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi E
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} E
                to one starting at
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi a
                        %mi b
                        %msup
                          %mi a
                          %mrow
                            %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                            %mn 1
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a b a^{-1}
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msup
                        %mrow
                        %mi a
                      %mi b
                      %mo ∈
                      %msub
                        %mi π
                        %mn 1
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi F
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} {}^a b \in \pi_1(F)
                (abusing notation by confusing paths and their homotopy classes). With this action
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %msub
                        %mi π
                        %mn 1
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi F
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo ,
                      %mi π
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi E
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo ,
                      %msub
                        %mi π
                        %mn 1
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi i
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (\pi_1(F), \pi(E), \pi_1(i))
                is a crossed module. This will not be proved here, but is not that difficult. (Of course, secretly, this example is ‘really’ the same as the previous one since a fibration of
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/simplicial+group"} simplicial groups
                is just morphism that is an
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/epimorphism"} epimorphism
                in each degree, and the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fibration+sequence"} fibre
                is thus just a
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/simplicial+group"} normal simplicial subgroup
            %li
              %p
                A particular case of this last example can be obtained from the inclusion of a subspace
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi A
                      %mo →
                      %mi X
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A\to X
                into a pointed space
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi X
                        %mo ,
                        %msub
                          %mi x
                          %mn 0
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (X,x_0)
                = succeed ")." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi x
                          %mn 0
                        %mo ∈
                        %mi A
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x_0\in A
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mover
                        %mi A
                        %mo ¯
                      %mo →
                      %mi X
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \overline{A}\to X
                in ‘the standard way’, and then find that the fundamental group of its fibre is
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi π
                          %mn 2
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi X
                        %mo ,
                        %mi A
                        %mo ,
                        %msub
                          %mi x
                          %mn 0
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \pi_2(X,A,x_0)
          %p A deep theorem of J.H.C. Whitehead is that the crossed module
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi δ
                  %mo :
                  %msub
                    %mi π
                    %mn 2
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi A
                  %mo ∪
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                  %msubsup
                    %mi e
                    %mi λ
                    %mn 2
                  %msub
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                    %mrow
                      %mi λ
                      %mo ∈
                      %mi Λ
                  %mo ,
                  %mi A
                  %mo ,
                  %mi x
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo →
                  %msub
                    %mi π
                    %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi A
                  %mo ,
                  %mi x
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \delta: \pi_2(A \cup \{e^2_\lambda\}_{\lambda \in \Lambda},A,x) \to \pi_1(A,x)
          %p
            is the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/free+crossed+module"} free crossed module
            on the characteristic maps of the
            = succeed "-cells." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn 2
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 2
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 1
            and
            = succeed ":" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn 2
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 2
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi σ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \sigma
            the formula
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi δ
                  %mi σ
                  %mo =
                  %mi a
                  %mo +
                  %mi b
                  %mo −
                  %mi a
                  %mo +
                  %mi b
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \delta \sigma = a+b-a +b
          %p
            makes sense with
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi σ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \sigma
            a generator of a free crossed module; in the usual abelian chain theory we can write only
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo ∂
                    %mi σ
                    %mo =
                    %mn 2
                    %mi b
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \partial \sigma =2b
          %p
            Whitehead’s proof of this theorem used knot theory and transversality. The theorem is also a consequence of the
            = succeed "-dimensional" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn 2
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 2
          %p
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi Π
                    %mn 2
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Pi_2
            \: (pairs of pointed spaces)
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo →
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \to
            (crossed modules)
          %p preserves certain colimits (see reference below).
          %p
            This last example was one of the first investigated by Whitehead and his proof appears also in a little book by
            = succeed ";" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hilton"} Hilton
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Nonabelian+algebraic+topology"} Nonabelian algebraic topology
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi π
                    %mn 2
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo ∪
                  %mi CA
                  %mo ,
                  %mi X
                  %mo ,
                  %mi x
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \pi_2(X \cup CA,X,x)
            as a crossed
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi π
                    %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo ,
                  %mi x
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \pi_1(X,x)
            module, and then Whitehead’s result is the case with
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            is a wedge of circles.
          %h2#related_concepts Related concepts
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lie+crossed+module"} Lie crossed module
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/internal+crossed+module"} internal crossed module
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/group"} group
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/2-group"}> 2-group
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %strong crossed module
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/differential+crossed+module"} differential crossed module
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/3-group"}> 3-group
                ,
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/2-crossed+module"} 2-crossed module
                \/
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/crossed+square"} crossed square
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/differential+2-crossed+module"} differential 2-crossed module
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/n-group"} n-group
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%E2%88%9E-group"}> ∞-group
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/simplicial+group"} simplicial group
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/crossed+complex"} crossed complex
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/hypercrossed+complex"} hypercrossed complex
          %h2#References References
          %p Historically, the second axiom for a crossed module first appeared as footnote 35 on p. 422 of:
          %ul
            %li#Whitehead41
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/J.H.C.+Whitehead"}> J.H.C. Whitehead
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %em On adding relations to homotopy groups
              %strong 42
              2 (1941) 409–428 &amp;lbrack;
              %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.2307/1968907"}> doi:10.2307/1968907
              \&amp;rbrack;;
          %p A key result on “Free crossed modules”.
          %ul
            %li#Whitehead49
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/J.+H.+C.+Whitehead"}> J. H. C. Whitehead
              , Section 16 of:
              = succeed "," do
                %em Combinatorial Homotopy II
              = succeed "&rbrack;" do
                %a{:href => "https://projecteuclid.org/journals/bulletin-of-the-american-mathematical-society/volume-55/issue-5/Combinatorial-homotopy-II/bams/1183513797.full"} euclid
          %p An exposition of this proof is in
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/R.+Brown"}> R. Brown
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %em On the second relative homotopy group of an adjunction space: an exposition of a theorem of J.H.C. Whitehead
              = succeed ")" do
                %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1112/jlms/s2-22.1.146"} doi:10.1112/jlms/s2-22.1.146
          %p see also
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Peter+J.+Hilton"}> Peter J. Hilton
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %em An Introduction to Homotopy Theory
              = succeed "&rbrack;" do
                %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1017/CBO9780511666278"} doi:10.1017/CBO9780511666278
          %p
            Note that the geometric core of the proof uses
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/knot+theory"} knot theory
            and transversality arguments which come from the “previous paper” of Whitehead:
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/J.+H.+C.+Whitehead"}> J. H. C. Whitehead
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %em On adding relations to homotopy groups
              %strong 42
              (1941) 400-428 &amp;lbrack;
              %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.2307/1968907"}> doi:10.2307/1968907
              \&amp;rbrack;
          %p
            Textbook account of crossed modules and their relation to
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/strict+2-groups"} strict 2-groups
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Saunders+MacLane"}> Saunders MacLane
              , §XII.8 of:
              = succeed "," do
                %em
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Categories+for+the+Working+Mathematician"} Categories for the Working Mathematician
              %strong 5
              Springer (second ed. 1997) &amp;lbrack;
              %a{:href => "https://link.springer.com/book/10.1007/978-1-4757-4721-8"}> doi:10.1007/978-1-4757-4721-8
              \&amp;rbrack;
          %p Further exposition:
          %ul
            %li#BaezLauda03
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/John+Baez"}> John Baez
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Aaron+Lauda"} Aaron Lauda
                = succeed "," do
                  %em HDA V: 2-Groups
                %strong 12
                (2004) 423-491. &amp;lbrack;
                %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/math.QA/0307200"}> arXiv:math.QA/0307200
                \&amp;rbrack;
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Johannes+Huebschmann"}> Johannes Huebschmann
                \:
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Crossed modules
                %strong 70
                (2023) 1802-1813 &amp;lbrack;
                %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/2403.15900"}> arXiv:2403.15900
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1090/noti2831"} doi:10.1090/noti2831
                = succeed "," do
                  %a{:href => "https://www.ams.org/journals/notices/202311/rnoti-p1802.pdf"} pdf
                = succeed "&rbrack;" do
                  %a{:href => "https://www.ams.org/notices/202311/202311FullIssue.pdf"} pdf
          %p Monograph:
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Friedrich+Wagemann"}> Friedrich Wagemann
              \:
              = succeed "," do
                %em Crossed Modules
              = succeed "," do
                %strong 82
              = succeed "&rbrack;" do
                %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1515/9783110750959"} doi:10.1515/9783110750959
          %p The following paper
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Ronnie+Brown"}> Ronnie Brown
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Philip+Higgins"} Philip Higgins
              = succeed "," do
                %em On the connections between the second relative homotopy groups of some related spaces
              %em Proc. London Math. Soc.
              (3) 36 (1978) 193-212.
          %p
            showed that the theorem of Whitehead on free crossed modules from CH II Sec 16 is a special case of a 2-dimensional Van Kampen type Theorem for the homotopy crossed modules (over groupoids) of open unions of “connected” triples
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo ,
                  %mi A
                  %mo ,
                  %mi S
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (X,A,S
            of spaces where
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi S
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S
            is a set of base points. However the proof of the main theorem uses the relation of crossed modules not to cat
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mo
                    %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} ^1
            \-groups but to “double groupoids with connections”, also proved with Spencer. Full details and references are in Part I of:
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Ronnie+Brown"}> Ronnie Brown
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Philip+Higgins"} Philip Higgins
              = succeed "," do
                %em
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Nonabelian+Algebraic+Topology"}> Nonabelian Algebraic Topology
                  \: Filtered spaces, Crossed Complexes, Cubical Homotopy Groupoids
          %p See also
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Ronnie+Brown"}> Ronnie Brown
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Groupoids and crossed objects in algebraic topology
            %li#Janelidze_03
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/George+Janelidze"}> George Janelidze
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Internal crossed modules
                %strong 10
                (2003) pp 99-114. (
                %a{:href => "https://eudml.org/doc/51553"}> EuDML
                )
          %p On crossed modules in other algebraic contexts:
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                A. S-T. Lue,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Cohomology of groups relative to a variety
                %strong 69
                1 (1981) 155–174
            %li
              %p
                Johan Leray, Salim Rivière,
                = succeed ":" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Friedrich+Wagemann"} Friedrich Wagemann
                %em Crossed modules and cohomology of algebras over an operad
                \&amp;lbrack;
                %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/2411.04614"}> arXiv:2411.04614
                \&amp;rbrack;
            %li
              %p
                Clovis Chabertier:
                %em Higher crossed modules of algebras over an operad
                \&amp;lbrack;
                %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/2412.03341"}> arXiv:2412.03341
                \&amp;rbrack;
              %blockquote
                %p
                  (in the generality of
                  = succeed ")" do
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/algebras+over+an+operad"} algebras over an operad
          %p
            Discussion of crossed modules
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/internalization"} internal to
            the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category"} category
            of
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lie-Rinehart+algebras"} Lie-Rinehart algebras
          %ul
            %li
              Joel Couchman,
              = succeed "," do
                %em Crossed modules and internal categories of Lie-Rinehart algebras
              = succeed "," do
                %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.25949/19439645.v1"} doi:10.25949/19439645.v1
              = succeed "&rbrack;" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/files/Couchman-CrossedModules.pdf", :title => "pdf"} pdf
